  <h2>Why pipes burst here</h2>

  <h3>Hard freezes</h3>
  <p>
    Baytown winters are mild, and that is the problem. Houses on the upper Texas coast are built for heat. Water lines often run through vented attics, exterior walls and garages with little insulation, because most years it does not matter.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then a year comes when it does. During the February 2021 winter outbreak, the National Weather Service recorded 13&deg;F in Houston, issued hard freeze warnings across the region for days, and described widespread power outages, loss of heat and broken pipes. With no power, homes could not be heated, pipes froze, and many split. The flooding started when things thawed and water began to flow again.
  </p>
  <p>Before a forecast hard freeze, the Red Cross recommends letting cold water drip from faucets served by exposed pipes, since moving water is harder to freeze. Other common steps are opening cabinet doors under sinks on outside walls, disconnecting garden hoses and covering outdoor faucets. If you will be away, or the power is out and the house is getting cold, shutting off the main and draining the lines removes the risk altogether.</p>

  <h3>Supply hoses and fittings</h3>
  <p>
    The flexible lines that feed toilets, faucets, ice makers, dishwashers and washing machines are under full pressure all day. Rubber washing machine hoses and plastic toilet supply nuts are frequent failures. Because a failed supply line runs until someone shuts it off, a break that happens while the family is at work or on vacation can soak several rooms.
  </p>

  <h3>Water heaters</h3>
  <p>
    Tank water heaters corrode from the inside and usually leak from the bottom. In many Baytown homes the heater sits in a garage, a hallway closet or the attic. An attic tank without a working drain pan can release its contents through the ceiling below.
  </p>

  <h3>Slab leaks</h3>
  <p>
    A large share of houses in this area are built on concrete slab foundations, with water lines running under or through the concrete. The clay soils common along the Texas Gulf Coast swell when wet and shrink when dry, and that movement can stress pipes over the years. A slab leak rarely looks like a burst pipe. Signs include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A water bill that climbs without explanation</li>
    <li>The sound of running water when every fixture is off</li>
    <li>A warm area on a tile or concrete floor (a hot water line)</li>
    <li>Damp carpet, cupping wood floors or loose tiles with no obvious source</li>
    <li>The water meter dial turning while nothing in the house is using water</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Finding and repairing a slab leak is plumbing work, often with leak detection equipment. The flooring and any wet walls still need to be dried afterward, and because slab leaks go unnoticed for a long time, mold is a common companion. See <a href="/mold-after-water-damage-baytown/">mold after water damage</a>.</p>

  <h3>Older piping</h3>
  <p>
    Baytown has housing from every decade since the oil boom, and older houses may still have galvanized steel supply lines or cast iron drains that have reached the end of their life. If you have had more than one leak in an older home, ask a licensed plumber whether repiping makes more sense than another patch.
  </p>

