  <h2>What drying looks like after a pipe break</h2>
  <p>
    Water from a clean supply line is the easiest kind to deal with if it is caught early. The restoration company will extract standing water, lift or remove carpet and pad as needed, pull baseboards, and set air movers and dehumidifiers. Wet insulation in walls or the attic usually has to come out, because it holds water against the framing.
  </p>
  <p>
    In our humid climate, crews normally dry with the house closed and dehumidifiers running, and they should check moisture levels on each visit. The full process is laid out on our <a href="/water-damage-restoration-baytown-tx/">water damage restoration page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Insurance notes for pipe breaks</h2>
  <p>
    The Texas Department of Insurance describes burst pipes and broken washer hoses as examples of the sudden, accidental water damage that most home policies cover, while damage from slow leaks and seepage is usually excluded. That distinction is why documentation matters: photos of the failed part, the date you found it, and a plumber's invoice describing the cause all help show what happened. Keep the broken part if you can. Our <a href="/water-damage-insurance-claim-tips/">claim tips page</a> has a checklist. We are not insurance professionals and cannot tell you what your policy will pay.
  </p>
